# Deploy update changelog

When you finish **user-visible** changes that will deploy to production (UI, UX, content, fixes to broken flows), **append** a short note to [gospel-admin/data/deploy-update-changelog.json](gospel-admin/data/deploy-update-changelog.json) (JSON string array, oldest first). Web and native users see every entry they have not read yet in **What's new** on return, and native users also see unseen entries in the **restart** alert when a deploy lands while the app is open.

**Keep only the last 5 entries** in the JSON file—drop the oldest when a sixth would be added. The API also serves at most the five most recent notes.

## When to update

- **Do update** for changes counselors or counselees might notice.
- **Do not update** for internal-only work (tests, refactors, CI, docs-only, admin tooling with no user impact).
- **Leave the array unchanged** when the release has nothing worth telling users.

## Writing rules

- Plain language for app users — not developers.
- No file paths, component names, APIs, “deploy”, “WebView”, “chunk”, or other technical terms.
- **1–3 short sentences** or up to **3 brief bullets** per entry; aim for **~200 characters** (server caps each entry at 400).
- Calm and helpful tone; not marketing hype.

## Examples

- Good: `Fixed an issue where the menu could open an external browser on iPhone.`
- Good: `Daily verse challenge: clearer feedback when you complete a day.`
- Bad: `CapacitorKeepLinksInApp touchstart intercept; router.push for same-origin links.`
- Bad: `Refactored capacitorDeployNotice controller and added Jest mocks.`

## File format

Append one string per user-facing release, e.g. `["Older note.", "This release note."]`. After appending, trim so the array has **at most 5 strings** (remove from the start). Shipped via `GET /api/app-deploy-version` as `changelog`.
